{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 26, :comments ["<b>And you shall make for it four rings of gold.</b> So that he remember that the successes of this world are a wheel that turns in the world like this circular ring, and through this remembrance he will provide from his table for the poor. And corresponding to this he will remember the greatness of the reward for the World to Come, which has no end, like this circular ring that has no end. And it says, ״לְעֻמַּת הַמִּסְגֶּרֶת תִּהְיֶיןָ הַטַּבָּעוֹת״ (Exodus 25:27), for the one who closes off his desires and there is an end to all his toil and is satisfied with what HaShem has granted him — he is the one who remembers that it is a wheel that turns in the world and shows grace to the poor, and he is the one who looks forward to the abundance of good hidden away for the righteous in the World to Come, which has neither beginning nor end. But the one who does not close off his craving and there is no end to all his toil and his eyes are never satisfied — he does not hope for the reward of the World to Come, nor does he ever give of his bread to the poor, as is well known from the way of most of our people. And this is also the meaning of the rings of the Ark, for through the Torah that is in the Ark one merits the circle of the righteous in Garden of Eden, like this circular form that has no end and resembles a ring. And for this reason the rings were round and not square." "And it is written here, ״לְבָתִּים לְבַדִּים״ (as housing for poles). Even though the rings of the Ark were also housings for poles, nevertheless this expression is not said there except in connection with the Table, because in the Table comes the allusion that the poor and the downtrodden — ״יָבִיא בָיִת״ (he will bring into a house; Isaiah 58:7). And this ״בָּתִּים לְבַדִּים״ — to the poor, who are called ״בַּדִּים״, for ״בַּד בְּבַד״ means solitary, and so every poor person dwells alone and everyone keeps away from him, as it says (Proverbs 19:7), ״אַף כִּי מֵרֵעֵהוּ רָחֲקוּ מִמֶּנּוּ״. And a sign for the matter: ״לְבַדִּים״ — the letters of ״בְּדָלִים״ (those who are separated). And just as these ״בַּדִּים״ (poles) are simply the bearers of the Table, so the poor are the bearers of the table of the rich, for in their merit HaShem sets a table for him, and ״נַחַת שֻׁלְחָנְךָ יִמָּלֵא דָשֶׁן״ (Job 36:16) — in the merit that he provides for the poor."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {1 "The wordplay on 'בדים' pivots on three senses: poles/bars (the literal bearing-poles of the Table), solitary/alone ('בד בבד'), and separated ('בדלים' as a rearrangement of letters). The translation preserves all three senses in sequence."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:23:20.548737148Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}